WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.100
THE STATE OF FREEMIUM:
Have you noticed that the YouTube experience

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.770
has been getting worse and worse for free
users?

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:12.010
This has been a trend since basically the
beginning of YouTube but 2023 has been especially

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.010
pivotal.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.870
It seems that every month, Google is trying
a new strategy to maximize how many ads viewers

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:18.870
see.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.779
For example, in May, YouTube started blocking
ad block, something that they had tolerated

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:25.330
for YouTube’s entire history.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.920
YouTube has even gone as far as disabling
video playback for people using ad block,

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.950
and that’s just the most obvious example.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:38.350
YouTube has also started experimenting with
30 second unskippable ads, and they’re even

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:44.129
moving to take some ad control away from creators,
presumably to optimize ad impressions and

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:45.450
maximize revenue.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:50.980
And it’s not just YouTube either, it seems
that every free service is undergoing a similar

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:51.980
evolution.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.450
Discord’s free stream quality, for example,
has fallen substantially over the past several

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:01.800
years, and don’t even get me started on
free mobiles games which are basically unplayable

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.570
at this point.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.170
The funny thing is that you can even argue
the same thing about paid services as well.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.490
Netflix’s lowest tier, for example, now
has ads.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:17.070
I don’t blame these platforms and I can’t
speak for everyone, but most of these free

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:21.960
platforms have offered be tremendous value
over the years whether its entertainment,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.830
education, or social connection.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:28.530
So, I think it’s completely fair for these
platforms to want to earn revenue for providing

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:29.950
this much value.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:34.750
But, it does make me question the viability
of freemium.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:39.759
Of course, freemium is a great avenue to break
barriers and make services more accessible

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:45.030
but should freemium really be the default
mode of monetization because it seems that

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.210
that just leads to the same dead end everytime.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:53.640
These platforms eventually get worse and worse
for free users until users either leave or

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:54.640
end up paying.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.310
So, join me as we take a look back at the
tech industries’ obsession with freemium

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:04.719
and why the days of freemium may finally be
coming to an end.

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:12.920
THE FREEMIUM DEBUT:
To understand how freemium became so popular,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.580
we first have to take a look back at what
convinced the tech industry about freemium

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:17.910
in the first place.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.410
At first glance, you might be inclined to
think that the culprit is Google or Facebook

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:28.209
or the slew of VC backed money burning startups,
but the culprit is actually none other than

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:29.209
Microsoft.

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:33.330
I’m of course talking about Windows and
Microsoft Office.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:39.150
Now this might be a bit confusing as neither
Windows nor Office was ever free but this

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.050
is how freemium used to work.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:45.560
The official software itself cost money, but
it was relatively easy to get the software

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.349
for free if you wanted to.

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:49.510
And Bill Gates was well aware of this.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:03:03.040
In fact, he openly admitted it and even sort
of encouraged it.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:17.580
Eventually, Microsoft did shift their focus
to monetizing users instead of growing users.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.230
This was primarily by getting computer manufacturers
as well as schools and employers to pay for

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.489
the software and this turned out to be a massive
success.

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:30.760
Microsoft is now the second largest company
in the world and sometimes even larger than

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.000
Apple.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.489
Companies like Google and Facebook as well
as a bunch of VCs saw this and looked to replicate

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:41.910
this formula in a bunch of other niches but
there was one crucial difference between what

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.519
Microsoft did and what all of these other
companies did.

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:50.890
With Microsoft, even though users never feel
the pain of directly paying, they are indeed

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:52.209
directly paying.

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:56.660
With new laptops and computers for example,
the price of the software is just directly

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.190
built into the cost of the machine itself.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:04.129
With schools, the cost of the software is
directly coming from tuition or local taxes.

00:04:04.129 --> 00:04:08.560
And with companies, the cost of the software
is directly coming from the bottom line which

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.010
just means that all employees will receive
slightly smaller salaries, lower bonuses,

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.260
you get the idea.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:18.549
The bottomline is that with Windows and Office,
while Microsoft has done a great job with

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:23.590
masking the cost, it’s still the end user
that’s paying for the software.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:29.510
With new age companies though, that’s not
the case which brings us into the first major

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:33.669
pitfall of freemium: the friction of monetization.

00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:38.510
The reason that YouTube’s monetization efforts
feel so significant is because there’s so

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:43.380
much friction when it comes to indirectly
monetizing our use of the platform.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:49.620
For example, let’s say that YouTube on average
earns $4 per 1000 views and that the average

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.080
view is worth 5 minutes of watch time.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.949
If you watch 1 hour of YouTube per day, you’re
averaging about 12 views per day or 4380 views

00:04:57.949 --> 00:04:59.530
per year.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:05.240
All of that translates to just $17.52 worth
of revenue for YouTube.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:11.740
Put another way, 365 hours of watch time is
not even worth 18 bucks.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:17.270
For perspective, subscribing to YouTube premium
from an iPhone costs more per month.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:22.690
Aka, YouTube would have to increase ads by
a factor of 12 to make casual users as lucrative

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.270
as paid users due to all the friction.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.520
Instead of directly earning revenue from users,
YouTube is having to wait for users to spend

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.979
money on advertisers products.

00:05:31.979 --> 00:05:36.729
Maybe 10% of this revenue gets put back into
marketing out of which 10% is actually spent

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:38.680
on YouTube marketing.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.660
From there, YouTube has to give about 50%
to creators meaning that at the end of the

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:49.210
day, YouTube is only left with max 0.5% of
the total revenue that their free users generate.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:54.949
Obviously, this is not all that great of a
monetization strategy but despite that, the

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:56.830
entire industry would jump in.

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:06.850
FREEMIUM RULES:
Whenever a given business works, other entrepreneurs

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:12.000
and VCs naturally try to reverse engineer
what made the business so successful.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.000
With companies like Microsoft, Google, and
Facebook, the answer seemed obvious.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.810
It was obviously the users that made these
businesses so successful.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:24.680
In reality, there was much more to it than
just that.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:30.430
For example, a solid strategy to monetize
these users is also equally important but

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:35.910
many businesses and investors would solely
fixate on just the first half of the equation:

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:36.910
users.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:41.370
If they could just dominate a given industry
in terms of market share, they could worry

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.550
about “collecting” later.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:49.699
One of the primary reasons that we got into
this vicious cycle of users over profits is

00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:51.740
due to low interest rates.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:56.000
With near zero interest rates, many investors
didn’t know where to put their money to

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.470
earn solid returns.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:02.440
So, they slowly put their money in riskier
and riskier investments leading to a startup

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:03.440
boom.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:10.139
I mean, just take a look at this graph, global
funding grew by 20x between 2006 and 2021.

00:07:10.139 --> 00:07:16.150
But, this insanity is finally starting to
slow down as investors now have a new place

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:17.560
to put their money: treasuries.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:22.190
If you’re not familiar with treasury bills
and bonds, it’s basically a way to help

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:23.770
fund our government.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:27.500
When you invest in treasuries, you’re basically
lending money to the government to help fund

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.050
public projects and social programs.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:33.780
And in return for helping the government fund
these programs, the government will pay you

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.910
back with interest of currently 5%.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:41.810
This is where the biggest corporations and
investors have been putting all of their cash

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:46.250
and we wanted to create an easy way for everyday
investors to do the same.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.810
And that’s why we created our bond investing
platform Silo.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:54.330
With Silo, you can invest in government and
corporate bill and bonds with the protection

00:07:54.330 --> 00:08:01.210
of $500,000 in SIPC insurance and $2.5 million
in FDIC insurance all from the comfort of

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:02.210
your phone.

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:07.139
If that sounds enticing, please consider checking
out Silo in the description below.

00:08:07.139 --> 00:08:13.160
But anyway, this strategy of users over profits
largely worked for much of the 2010s.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:18.120
As long as companies were able to show phenomenal
user growth month over month and year over

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:22.620
year, their valuations would keep increasing,
they would be able to raise more money and

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.270
everybody would be happy.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:28.840
The only problem with this strategy though
is that if you pursue it for long enough,

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:31.840
you eventually just run out of users.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.390
Everyone who could possibly use your app or
service is already using it.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.490
For most tech companies, this happened right
after Covid hit.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:43.599
With everyone stuck at home, these companies
experienced a final surge before achieving

00:08:43.599 --> 00:08:45.250
market saturation.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:50.310
And now all of sudden, their abysmal revenue
per user actually matters.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.769
Likely the best example of this is WhatsApp.

00:08:52.769 --> 00:08:58.720
There’s no question that WhatsApp is virtually
ubiquitous with over 2 billion active users.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.050
But, the same cannot be said about their revenue.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:06.770
In fact, WhatsApp doesn’t even generate
a billion dollars in annual revenue despite

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.990
controlling a quarter of the world population.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:14.340
And a lot of this just has to do with the
fact that there is no straight forward way

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.440
to monetize a messaging application.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.610
And this brings us into the next major pitfall
of freemium which is that it trains users

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.980
to expect these services for free.

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:29.680
WhatsApp used to be free to use till yesterday,
so why can’t it just keep being free?

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:34.450
YouTube used allow AdBlock till yesterday,
so why can’t they just keep allowing it?

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:40.339
The answer is that doing so is not only expensive
but quite unprofitable, and the only reason

00:09:40.339 --> 00:09:44.110
these companies put up with it for so long
is to hopefully convince you that the given

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:46.519
platform is of value.

00:09:46.519 --> 00:09:51.700
Most users are aware of this but at the same
time, they don’t care either and I mean

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:52.700
why should they?

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:57.440
It’s not their responsibility to worry about
whether Google or Meta is making money.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:01.300
All that matters to them is how much value
they’re getting from a platform or service

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:06.230
and when that perceived value goes down due
to more ads or higher subscription fees, they’re

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:07.750
naturally gonna be upset.

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:12.260
If anything, it’s these companies’ fault
for setting the expectation that these services

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:13.540
are free.

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:20.010
They went along with it because it was beneficial
in the short term but the same cannot be said

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:26.600
about the long term and that brings us into
the current crisis that much of tech is facing:

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:33.380
the need to monetize.

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:36.520
THE RACE TO MONETIZE:
The bottomline is that the party is over.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:41.830
Whether you’re startup that has relied on
VC funding or you’re a tech giant like Google,

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:45.639
the days of putting user growth over profits
are behind us.

00:10:45.639 --> 00:10:50.050
These companies are moving to monetize as
quickly as possible and they’re having to

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:56.010
face all of the issues that they had previously
ignored like the friction of indirectly monetizing

00:10:56.010 --> 00:11:00.220
users and users’ unwillingness to suddenly
pay for things that they have been using for

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.829
free for literally decades.

00:11:02.829 --> 00:11:07.600
And all of this is very much painting these
companies as the villain.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:12.980
Ah, it’s so annoying that Netflix is raising
prices and cracking down on password sharing.

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:17.880
It’s so annoying that YouTube is blocking
AdBlock and stuffing in more ads, and so on

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:19.120
and so forth.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:25.149
These companies can of course continue to
experiment with more creative modes of monetization

00:11:25.149 --> 00:11:30.839
but honestly, the better solution is probabaly
just ditch the freemium model.

00:11:30.839 --> 00:11:36.520
The reality is that for many of these platforms
to truly be viable with free users, the user

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:43.510
experience would have to be degraded by such
a ridiculous amount that it makes no sense.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.720
Take YouTube for example.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:49.310
Let’s say YouTube wants to earn just $5
per user per month.

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:52.380
I think most of you would agree that that’s
a reasonable amount.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.300
For YouTube to achieve this with the average
user who watches let’s say 1 hour of YouTube

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:02.639
per day, YouTube would have to show about
10 minutes worth of ads every hour and that’s

00:12:02.639 --> 00:12:04.680
assuming that they earn $4 per 1000 views.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:09.850
In most niches, that’s not the case meaning
that YouTube would have to show even more

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:12.170
ads like 15 to 20 minutes per hour.

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:17.280
I don’t know about you but that doesn’t
seem all that enticing to me and I suspect

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:20.910
that these companies are coming to the exact
same realization.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:25.910
To earn any sort of meaningful revenue from
free users, they would basically have to destroy

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:31.190
the platform with ads or data collection or
payment for order flow or whatever new age

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:33.300
mode of monetization they’re using.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:38.190
As such, I suspect that the main reason that
YouTube is cracking down so hard on adblock

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:43.790
and pushing ads is not to better monetize
free users but rather to simply push free

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:49.420
users to become paid users as I think that
this will become the new norm.

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:53.550
The new norm will not be freemium, the new
norm will be paid.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:57.930
These platforms will of course keep around
their free versions but I suspect that these

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:04.029
will be more like lite versions that are watered
down and noticeably worse than the paid version.

00:13:04.029 --> 00:13:08.670
This would obviously be a massive shift from
what we’ve been used to over the past 2

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:12.490
decades but hopefully it’ll be for the better.

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:17.810
With users directly paying for these platforms,
hopefully we can move past more shady modes

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:23.300
of monetization like data collection and payment
for order flow and things of that nature.

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:28.630
Or who knows, maybe companies will just end
up doing both and things get worse than ever.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:32.089
Are you fan of freemium or would you rather
pay for your services?

00:13:32.089 --> 00:13:33.649
Comment that down below.

00:13:33.649 --> 00:13:38.860
Also, it seems that all of those money burning
startups who just focused on growth are currently

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.839
getting their funding pulled.

00:13:40.839 --> 00:13:45.769
Check out this video to learn more.

